WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.230
- And just like on their
show, sometimes on our show,

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:05.010
we have stories so long and epic

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:06.780
that they need their own segments.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.370
Ed has one of those stories today.

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:11.439
So here's another installment
of Epic Storytime.

00:00:11.439 --> 00:00:12.712
(upbeat melody)

00:00:12.712 --> 00:00:16.100
♪ Epic Storytime ♪

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.590
So how this works is, I ask one question

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:20.710
and Ed tells Randall and me his story

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:23.530
with the help of the book
of illustrations he has.

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:26.140
So here's my one question to start.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:28.623
Hey Ed, speaking of National Soup Month,

00:00:28.623 --> 00:00:29.456
(chuckles)

00:00:29.456 --> 00:00:30.850
'cause that's something you'd say,

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:31.930
what's,
(chuckles)

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:34.713
what's one of your most
memorable soup experiences?

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:37.200
- Great question.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.140
And it's a doozy.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:39.973
- [kelly Clarkson] Okay.

00:00:39.973 --> 00:00:44.060
- I once had a soup experience
with Francis Ford Coppola.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:45.370
Are you ready?
- What? Yes.

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:46.560
- [Ed Helms] Okay.
- Jealous.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:47.490
- [Ed Helms] All right.

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:48.323
- [kelly Clarkson] Okay.

00:00:48.323 --> 00:00:49.970
- And here's the beautiful book

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:51.870
that I've been working on all weekend.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.320
So this was back in my early twenties,

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.190
in my early twenties I
used to have very long hair

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:59.460
and I had big mutton chops and,

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:00.293
- [kelly Clarkson] Attractive.

00:01:00.293 --> 00:01:01.750
- Yeah, and I wore a peacoat.

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:04.350
Like I was very much
like an indie film dork.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:06.470
Now, I was living in New
York city at the time.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:11.470
And there was an article
in the New York Post

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:14.680
that there were gonna be open auditions

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:18.020
for Francis Ford Coppola's adaptation

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.800
of Jack Kerouac's "On the Road."

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.170
So my buddy Mark Webb,

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:25.000
who's actually a big time
director out here now.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.260
He and I were like, "Okay, we're
gonna go to this audition."

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.680
And so it turns out
it's at this big church

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:34.500
in Midtown Manhattan and it's
like, it's freezing cold.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.160
We go there, and one of the things

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:39.280
that the article said was,
when you go to the audition,

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.490
bring a headshot and a cassette tape

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:47.490
of yourself reading a Jack
Kerouac poem or something.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:48.433
- [kelly Clarkson] Okay.

00:01:48.433 --> 00:01:49.940
- So we did that. I
didn't have any headshots.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:53.514
So I had my aunt take some pictures of me.

00:01:53.514 --> 00:01:54.426
(all laugh)

00:01:54.426 --> 00:01:55.259
- [Man] Oh no.

00:01:55.259 --> 00:01:59.557
- And they turned out like
snapshots that your aunt took.

00:01:59.557 --> 00:02:00.390
(all laugh)

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.034
That's basically what my
headshots looked like.

00:02:02.034 --> 00:02:03.730
(chuckles)

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:08.730
But yes. Anyway, so off we go
to the audition in Midtown,

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:10.450
it's about 15 degrees out.

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:13.870
It's so cold and we get
there and there's a huge line

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:16.120
that's wrapped all the
way around the block.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:17.890
Lots of hours of waiting ahead.

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:21.330
So we get in line and we just hunker down.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:23.280
And at first, like no one's
really talking to each other

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:24.870
'cause it's just cold and
you're just out there,

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:27.300
but hours go by and you get
to know everyone in line

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.490
around you, and you're kind
of like going through this

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.167
traumatic experience together.

00:02:31.167 --> 00:02:32.920
(all chuckle)
So everybody's, bonding

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:35.320
and you're turning into
like very close friends.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:40.810
We're getting close to the
entrance and we're so cold.

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:43.370
Like we are just not
prepared for this weather.

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:44.960
We didn't know we'd have to wait outside.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:49.960
It's been hours, a PA
comes out with a tray

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:52.840
with hot chicken noodle soup,

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:55.030
and just starts handing it
out to everyone in line.

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:55.863
- [Randall Park] That's nice.

00:02:55.863 --> 00:02:56.696
- That's nice.

00:02:56.696 --> 00:02:59.610
- That is the kind of person
Francis Ford Coppola is.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:00.443
Like weird,

00:03:00.443 --> 00:03:01.276
- [kelly Clarkson] I'm
like, that's really nice.

00:03:01.276 --> 00:03:02.610
- Yes, and that right away, I was like,

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:04.235
he's a great director.

00:03:04.235 --> 00:03:05.068
(all chuckle)

00:03:05.068 --> 00:03:07.100
Yeah, I mean, he's made some good movies,

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:08.023
but also he gave us chicken noodle soup.

00:03:08.023 --> 00:03:11.170
- Your work is fine, but
you cared that we didn't die

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:12.734
while trying to audition for this.

00:03:12.734 --> 00:03:13.880
- Yeah, we got hot chicken noodle soup.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:17.620
So we're like, savoring our
Francis Ford Coppola soup,

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:19.310
- He probably hated himself.
- Which presumably he,

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:21.010
(all chuckle)
- Yeah, yeah.

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:22.820
And it really got everybody's spirits up

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:24.550
and we're almost there.

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:27.680
And we finally get into,

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:31.130
they sort of funnel you
into this church basement.

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:34.650
And what we see is a row of tables

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:36.360
and there are like casting directors

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.890
sitting behind these tables.

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:41.330
And literally all you do is walk up

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:44.150
and hand them your headshot
and your cassette tape.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:45.643
And we're like,

00:03:46.747 --> 00:03:48.410
"That's fucking it?"

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:50.821
- Wait, what?
- Like, "That's all you do?"

00:03:50.821 --> 00:03:51.654
(background laughter)

00:03:51.654 --> 00:03:53.054
Yeah, yeah.

00:03:53.054 --> 00:03:53.887
- [Kelly Clarkson] What?

00:03:53.887 --> 00:03:54.720
Okay. Well, so,

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:55.940
- Where you upset?

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:58.980
- Well we're kinda like, I mean,

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:02.760
here's the, the other detail
is that behind the tables,

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:06.280
is Francis Ford Coppola,
pacing back and forth.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:09.130
And as I go up to hand my headshot

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:11.010
and cassette to a casting director,

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:14.100
Francis Ford Coppola comes
out from behind the tables,

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.840
shakes my hand and says,
"Thanks for coming."

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:20.470
I was like, "You're welcome."

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:21.340
- What?

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:22.640
- And then we leave.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:25.470
The next day we read in the paper

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:29.500
that there was a secret code
that Francis Ford Coppola had

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:30.500
with the casting directors.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:32.970
If he goes out and shakes your hand,

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:34.900
then they put your
headshot and cassette tape

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:38.437
in a separate pile for
further consideration.

00:04:38.437 --> 00:04:39.353
- [kelly Clarkson] What?

00:04:39.353 --> 00:04:40.757
- Yeah.

00:04:40.757 --> 00:04:42.509
- How great did you feel? Oh, my God.

00:04:42.509 --> 00:04:45.360
- So you didn't book the part per se

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:48.420
but you booked the
audition, you won him over.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:50.490
- Okay, let's not get ahead of ourselves.

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.002
- Okay, okay. Sorry, sorry.

00:04:52.002 --> 00:04:54.050
- 'Cause the story ain't over.

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:54.883
- Okay.

00:04:54.883 --> 00:04:57.513
- All right? Basically,

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:01.650
I still haven't heard,

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:03.970
(Kelly and Randall laugh)

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.040
I'm still holding by the phone.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:07.340
- [kelly Clarkson] Not sure.

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:09.340
- And I'm just still holding out.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:12.430
I think, to my knowledge, he
hasn't made the movie yet,

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:13.263
- [Ed Helms] Yeah.
- [Kelly Clarkson] Okay.

00:05:13.263 --> 00:05:15.660
- So I might still get it.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:17.320
I am waiting for that call

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:18.153
- [kelly Clarkson] Oh, my God.

00:05:18.153 --> 00:05:19.087
- When Francis will call and say,

00:05:19.087 --> 00:05:22.560
"Ed, I finally made it
through all the tapes,

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:23.740
yours was amazing."

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:25.090
- He's been listening to the tapes.

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:26.696
- Let's go get some soup.

00:05:26.696 --> 00:05:27.529
- Boom,
(all clap)

00:05:27.529 --> 00:05:29.980
Francis Ford Coppola shook my hand.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:33.520
A big thank you to Marcela Acevedo

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:35.410
in our art department for those drawings.

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:37.450
He's incredible, we love them.

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:39.300
And thank you so much
for coming up here, guys.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:41.470
I loved having Cafe Alfresco with you.

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:42.883
- So fun, thank you.
- [kelly Clarkson] Yes.

